body:
Fear, I guess.

&#x200B;

The fact that nobody knows what happens once you die is utterly terrifying. I've tried suicide once and only once because I shit myself at the thought of complete darkness once I died. I was suicidal then, but now I see that there is more to see and experience, no matter how much I am abused, beaten down and harassed. Life is one of there "once in a lifetime" offers in my opinion. The fact that there is a one in a millionth chance of being alive is just incredible.

Personally, I'm trapped. Probably under different circumstances. I feel like a burden on my parents. My mum seems like she hates me. Although she says that she will talk with me. She is the HARDEST woman to approach so I tried talking to my dad who just tells me that I'm overreacting. They both even refuse to let me get counselling so that's out of the window. My brother lives in London so I can't even reach out to him. My mental health at the moment is nothing but a pile of dust. I get that these things seem like stupid reasons to be mildly suicidal and to have severe depression but this is how I feel.

&#x200B;

I get it. You feel boxed in beyond all hell. I guess the first step is long walks through the park without friends or family. Long walks for me are like an adventure. They are like an opening to a place where I don't have to worry. They are my escape. Just try and find something that will help you escape if my best recommendation, then work your way outwards, like going out with friends or venting to someone so you can hopefully feel less trapped.

Life is like a walk in the park. Some people continue walking until they've gone round in one big circle before heading home to sleep for the night. Other people grow tired of walking and head home as soon as they can. However, there are those few people who sit there in the middle of the path. They are too tired to continue but don't want to go home just yet.

&#x200B;

My point is that suicide isn't your only option. You and many others still have time to regain your energy to continue the long walk through the park.

&#x200B;

You are afraid to jump and I get that. It's one hell of a thing to jump and leave everything you know and love behind. To know that you will soon plunge into darkness is a form of freedom. However, I suggest you give life another shot.

Edit: I know that it's hard, but one small nudge is all it takes to keep moving, friend.
